BEGIN_PGML


Formal proofs that an equation can be locally solved (or not) are quite tricky since there are a lot of technical details. Here are various proofs related to the curve [` x^2=y^3 `]. See this [@ htmlLink("https://www.desmos.com/calculator/alib79jrrn", "Desmos graph") @]*.

>>[@ image("e3-cusp.png", width=>400) @]*<<

Asif has a poorly written proof that [` x^2-y^3=0 `] locally defines [` x `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` y `] near [` (1,1) `].

---

1. _Let [` V = (0,\infty) `] and [` W = (0,\infty) `]._
2. _Define [` \phi : V \to W `] by [` \phi(y) = y^{3/2} `]._
3. _Notice [` x^2- y^{3}=0 `] if and only if [` x = y^{3/2} = \phi(y)`]._
4. _Thus, [` x^2-y^3=0 `] locally defines [` x `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` y `] near [` (1,1) `]._

---

There is no fatal error, but each of the first 3 lines could use a bit more detail. Add those details.

[_]{$mc1}

Fabian gives an argument that [` x^2-y^3=0 `] does _not_ locally define [` y `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` x `] near [` (0,0) `].

---

1. _Suppose for a contradiction that there exists a [` C^1 `] function [` \phi : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} `] such that_

>>[` \{ (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : x^2-y^3 = 0 \} = \{ (x,\phi(x)) : x \in \mathbb{R} \} `].<<

2. _This implies for [` x \in \mathbb{R} `] that [` x^2 - [\phi(x)]^3 = 0 `] so [` [\phi(x)]^3 = x^2 `]._
3. _Then [` \phi(x) = x^{2/3} `] but [` \phi `] is not differentiable at [` 0 `]._
4. _Thus, [` \phi `] is not [` C^1`], a contradiction._

---

The proof has a flaw. Identify the flaw and suggest how to fix it.

[_]{$mc2}

Semeon has argued that [` x^2-y^3=0 `] does _not_ locally define [` x `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` y `] near [` (0,0) `].

---

1. _Let [` V, W \subseteq \mathbb{R} `] be open subsets containing [`0`], so [` W \times V `] contains [` (0,0) `]._
2. _Suppose for a contradiction that there exists a [` C^1 `] function [` \phi: V \to W `] such that_

>>[` \{ (x,y) \in W \times V : x^2-y^3 = 0 \} = \{ (\phi(y),y) : y \in V \} `].<<

3. _This implies for [` y \in V `] that [` [\phi(y)]^2 - y^3 = 0 `] so [` \phi(y) = y^{3/2} `]._
4. _Then [` \phi(y) = y^{3/2} `] but [` \phi `] is not differentiable at [` 0 `]._
5. _Thus, [` \phi `] is not [` C^1 `], a contradiction._

---

His proof has a serious flaw. Identify it and fix his proof.

[_]{$mc3}
END_PGML

